---
title: CI
description: Workflow triggers, the verify jobs, and the weekly snapshot refresh pull request
---

# CI

Three workflows run under `.github/workflows/`. The verify workflow gates every pull request. The agent eval runs only on request. The snapshot refresh runs weekly and opens a pull request a person merges.

## Layer responsibilities

- `verify.yml`: runs on every pull request and every push to `main`, with one job per language root
- `agent-eval.yml`: runs on `workflow_dispatch` only, sending the cases in `web/evals/` to a chosen provider
- `snapshot-refresh.yml`: runs every Monday, regenerates the mock service responses the web tests read, and opens a pull request when they changed
- `scripts/verify.sh`: the same cascade a developer runs locally with `bun run check`

The jobs in `verify.yml` call the same scripts a developer runs, so a local pass predicts a CI pass apart from the network.

## Verify jobs

Each job installs only its own root's dependencies.

### Root checks

- Format check over the whole tree with prettier and shfmt
- Spell check with cspell
- Shell lint with shellcheck at warning level
- Commit message lint on the pull request's commits

### Python checks

- `uv sync --frozen` from `python/`
- Ruff lint and format check
- Mypy in strict mode over `python/src/`
- Pytest with the network blocked, so a test reaching the internet fails rather than flakes

### Web checks

- `bun install --frozen-lockfile` from `web/`
- ESLint and the TypeScript compiler with no emit
- Vitest over unit and component tests
- A route test that runs the chat route against the mock service snapshots

## Decisions

### The agent eval runs on request only

A full eval run costs real tokens on a hosted provider. Running it on every pull request would spend more in a week than the project costs to host in a year. A maintainer starts it from the Actions tab and picks the provider.

### Snapshot refresh lands as a bot pull request

The mock responses come from the real service run against a fixed database. The workflow regenerates them and opens a pull request rather than committing to `main`, so a person reads the diff before a changed response reaches the web tests.

## Hidden contracts

- The snapshot refresh writes to `web/test/snapshots/`. The web tests read that folder by path.
- `verify.yml` job names are required checks on `main`. Renaming one leaves the ruleset waiting on a check that never reports.
- The eval workflow reads the provider key from a repository secret named after the provider in upper case.

## Gotchas

### The refresh job formats with `bunx`

The refresh runs in a job that installs no root dependencies, so it runs prettier through `bunx prettier` rather than the root script. A change to the root prettier version needs the same pin in the workflow.
